<section id="tasks"><h2>Can the model choose a motion that works?</h2>
<p>We give a planner a target and a fixed set of commands. It forecasts each command, selects one, and sends it to Newton. A pass counts only when the executed motion meets the task tolerances. This measures the consequence of choosing the wrong action, even when two predicted shapes look similar.</p>
<h3>Fold a cloth and let go</h3>
<figure><picture><source media="print, (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)" srcset="tasks/cloth/cloth-fold.png"><img src="tasks/cloth/cloth-fold.gif" width="900" height="340" alt="Newton and the folding surrogate execute the same two-corner grasp, carry, release and settle on the first confirmation fabric."></picture><figcaption>First confirmation fabric. The two colors follow the original halves of the cloth as they overlap. The motion on the left is the execution used for scoring. <a href="tasks/cloth/cloth-fold.gif">Open folding animation</a>.</figcaption></figure>
<p>A new cloth expert learns the motion of all 117 vertices from 200 examples. Its inputs describe the fabric and the prescribed corner motion. After every grasp releases, success requires an aligned fold, limited height and low terminal speed. The expert takes 0.24 seconds to fit on one CPU after 32.6 seconds of Newton data generation on ten workers. It has its own weights and a fixed grasp schedule.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Cloth. Each planner receives the same 16 fresh fabric/target pairs and the same bank of 12 grasp programs. Times are means on four of those tasks, measured on one warmed CPU core. They cover planning, before executing the final choice.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Planner</th><th scope="col" class="num">Newton checks</th><th scope="col" class="num">Successful folds</th><th scope="col" class="num">Planning time</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for key, title, shots, timing in fold_rows %}<tr><td>{{ title }}</td><td class="num">{{ shots }}</td><td class="num">{{ fold[key].successes }} / 16</td><td class="num">{{ timing }}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>
<p>The learned shortlist and three Newton checks gave {{ fold.neural_plus_newton.successes }} successful folds, one fewer than searching all twelve, at {{ "%.2f"|format(fold_clock.full_over_hybrid) }}× the planning speed. The model alone predicted intersecting final layers on 11 of 16 selected programs. All 16 hybrid-selected Newton executions finished with correctly ordered sampled layers and zero strict nonadjacent edge–face crossings. A four-fabric refinement check changed two original binary outcomes. The tests use ideal corner grasps and exclude robot grasp acquisition. <a href="https://github.com/maxkra15/supernerd/tree/main/tasks/cloth">Folding model and reproduction</a> · <a href="tasks/cloth/confirmation.json">Individual outcomes</a> · <a href="tasks/cloth/timings.json">Timing samples</a>.</p>
<p><strong>When should Newton take over?</strong> An optional planner checks the fabric and every proposed grasp program against the coordinate ranges observed during training. Outside those ranges it skips neural prediction and checks the whole bank. On the same 16 fabrics it used the model on {{ guarded.used_model_cases }}, executed {{ guarded.native_evaluations }} Newton checks in total, and achieved {{ guarded.successes }}/16 folds. Always using the neural shortlist took 48 checks; full search took 192. This retrospective test measures how much work the fallback sends to Newton. The separate 3.91× timing above covers the original three-check planner. <a href="tasks/cloth/guarded-confirmation.json">Per-case routing and executed checks</a> · <a href="https://github.com/maxkra15/supernerd/blob/main/tasks/cloth/guarded_planner.py">Support-aware planner</a>.</p>
<h3>Steer a flexible cable through two timed gates</h3>
<p>Eight new clamped cables each receive four goals. The planner chooses two smooth tip loads. To pass, the Newton tip must land within 10 mm of each requested lateral position at 1.10 and 2.40 seconds. The shared SuperNeRD and NeurALL models use their existing weights. This task exercises free-space control under load.</p>
<figure><picture><source media="print, (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)" srcset="tasks/cable/cable-gates-success.png"><img src="tasks/cable/cable-gates-success.gif" alt="The same two-pulse command shown in Newton and the mixed SuperNeRD model, with lateral tip position at both target times."></picture><figcaption>One passing example, chosen as the first pass among the first goals on each cable. The plot exposes each gate tolerance. The first case in seed order misses the target. <a href="tasks/cable/cable-gates-success.gif">Passing motion</a> · <a href="tasks/cable/cable-gates.gif">First case and its miss</a>.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Cable. Identical 25 candidate programs, scored on 32 goals from eight independent geometries. The right column times a specialized calculation of the two requested tip positions on one CPU core. These measurements exclude physical execution.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Planner</th><th scope="col" class="num">Both gates passed</th><th scope="col" class="num">Mean worst gate error</th><th scope="col" class="num">Two-gate readout</th></tr></thead><tbody>
{% for key, title in cable_rows %}<tr><td>{{ title }}</td><td class="num">{{ gates[key].passed }} / 32</td><td class="num">{{ "%.1f"|format(gates[key].mean_max_gate_distance_mm) }} mm</td><td class="num">{% if key in gate_fast %}{{ "%.2f"|format(gate_fast[key].fast_ms) }} ms{% else %}—{% endif %}</td></tr>{% endfor %}
</tbody></table></div>
<p>SuperNeRD can evaluate its damped modes at just the two gate times, which preserves every command decision in this test. NeurALL has lower mean worst-gate error; its recurrent state advances through the intervening times. In a continuous-action follow-up, a three-probe Newton controller reached all 32 targets in {{ "%.2f"|format(gate_cont.methods.newton_three_probes.planner_s) }} seconds of planning per goal. With two Newton probes, both a simple controller and its SuperNeRD-assisted counterpart completed 26 of 32 goals. The learned correction gave no extra passes at that budget.</p>
<p>Doubling Newton’s substeps and iteration count shifted the generating command’s worst gate by {{ "%.1f"|format(gate_cont.stock_refined_witness_mean_worst_gate_shift_mm) }} mm on average. None of the 32 generating commands still passed both original gates. This makes the specified Newton configuration part of the cable task. Physical 10 mm accuracy requires a better-qualified teacher. <a href="https://github.com/maxkra15/supernerd/tree/main/tasks/cable">Cable protocol and models</a> · <a href="tasks/cable/results/summary.json">Task outcomes</a> · <a href="tasks/cable/results/fast-planning.json">Readout parity and timings</a> · <a href="tasks/cable/results/continuous-summary.json">Continuous control and refinement</a>.</p>
<h3>Where sharing helps</h3><p>SuperNeRD and NeurALL now run on identical cable commands and face the same Newton execution test. A fixed-grasp folding expert adds a different action interface. A future robot task would also need moving grasp frames, contact geometry, and a plug or gripper pose where the task depends on orientation. Sharing task definitions, actions and execution metrics lets specialized models compete while preserving which inputs and physics each one supports. A new model can submit its selected action for every case through one public scorer. <a href="https://github.com/maxkra15/supernerd/tree/main/tasks#evaluate-a-deformable-model-on-actions">Submit and score action choices</a>.</p></section>
